#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092028 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b03546f-6c79-53b9-b284-fb23ae4dba52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "1957007f-eabd-5d40-8f59-ff635ea1d539" :question/math-academy-id "e-602" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039829/assets/834eff403ac2a6f696e104ec914cac1b951f8a6d989e11c0e9f2576ac9e7f0d8.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-t, \\quad y= \\dfrac{2}{3} t^{3/2}, \\qquad t\\in[0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2})={t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(-1)}^{2}+{({t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092029 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "86835544-a7f1-5be2-99c1-14e39a14f3fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-603" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=e^t(\\cos{t}+\\sin{t}), \\quad y=e^t(\\cos{t}-\\sin{t}) , \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right].\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039829/assets/15d185178bf8882f837c19361d630c0e537e145ca2337cad4655677255d0348a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Computing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092060 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "5bae6269-2112-56ea-b123-bee58701cf87" :question/math-academy-id "e-7130" :question/problem "Find the expression that gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t, \\, y=\\ln{(\\cos{t})}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (see graph below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039829/assets/48de26c11ae08afb45ec19ee56e770f7418f9be2afb8ee7dd46a0d1136cb125b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eaba7bba-53b5-5509-b334-f4da84149b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304306} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab95e35-7027-5a36-acd6-ca913286129c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304307} {:answer/id #uuid "e20f86aa-e684-58e6-92cf-1a4c7135d75c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304308} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d7eb29-18e9-5b97-949e-7bc4e9c60919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304309} {:answer/id #uuid "7bbbd4b1-18d0-55a9-a077-1f69d804375e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ab95e35-7027-5a36-acd6-ca913286129c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304307} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce0eaa8c-5036-522e-97ea-b1d5ec6ad207" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd387a77-72ef-5833-83c0-73cdf3cd884d" :question/math-academy-id "q-10257" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y=2{sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{cos}^{3}⁡t)=-6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{sin}^{3}⁡t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(-6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(6{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{cos}^{4}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "445be6a6-0063-502b-9aac-23e37c7a323e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304313} {:answer/id #uuid "abb451af-ba20-5d14-9ac4-af61b67a6cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304314} {:answer/id #uuid "79ea321c-fe00-5a87-a86d-00591936b716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304315} {:answer/id #uuid "1357c54c-84b7-5d6b-992f-79e653977c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304316} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe551f7-24b9-5809-b76f-56a3d8f97901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79ea321c-fe00-5a87-a86d-00591936b716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304315} :answer-field/id #uuid "38bd5068-041d-5e13-96a4-7dbcc59c88b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "954304bc-c5f4-5131-95ba-54e09bfed3c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-10261" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039829/assets/8288453c2625dac592a51054df332abf8ee49dbf8cbcabaf327c3ffe63b55f71.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4+4-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$ Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{t}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{t}{2}) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =16{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-8(0-1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b03546f-6c79-53b9-b284-fb23ae4dba52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b70caea-1ccf-5a45-8d78-16c3fffa43ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+5t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304320} {:answer/id #uuid "5a48e08e-e8d9-5871-ad12-b6c74ffa63ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}-12}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304321} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa61ac9-4138-50d5-8a79-1de8485c52c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304322} {:answer/id #uuid "10925a1f-1d5a-52d0-aee3-8dbfdb5ef588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304323} {:answer/id #uuid "7eac3d80-f3c0-5717-9e97-4e428b9ceb08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{6{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10925a1f-1d5a-52d0-aee3-8dbfdb5ef588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304323} :answer-field/id #uuid "d37965b3-61d6-5169-9b49-be5a394abde7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9091f4f0-0534-58df-a783-a69125c7f605" :question/math-academy-id "q-123019" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039829/assets/0a58808ee9b20e201d1ea8a31683f742e02f739127640de2fea87ce4757e7901.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=3{t}^{2},\\,y=5t$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})=6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t)=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{(6t)^{2}+(5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b03546f-6c79-53b9-b284-fb23ae4dba52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e0933ca-ba48-5db9-82fb-7ffa2d7eadc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304327} {:answer/id #uuid "b3aa40dd-e4c5-519e-ac8d-0e5b89f0a87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304328} {:answer/id #uuid "bf250aec-9cb7-5333-bfed-dbbf13f3542f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304329} {:answer/id #uuid "661f9314-797d-5e07-ba5c-4970f63fe671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304330} {:answer/id #uuid "aab887c4-9bef-52b7-948c-d8ce8dfad46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{3{t}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3aa40dd-e4c5-519e-ac8d-0e5b89f0a87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304328} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ab018a4-0d7e-59a4-856b-d31b5a0e5be9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce632c36-0a89-59ca-a425-8c378bad7972" :question/math-academy-id "q-123020" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039829/assets/ae210abc4028440aa9c822510d68f0ab0b8ba4b9f5806dd299149e953ccd1b21.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={t}^{3}-4,\\,y=2t$\nfrom $t=-2$ to $t=0$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-4)=3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2})^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b03546f-6c79-53b9-b284-fb23ae4dba52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a22c9cb-e3d1-5ce7-9d7e-0f9c1911b806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+{t}^{2}-5t+6}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304334} {:answer/id #uuid "f17eafaa-4e17-52c4-80d4-1de331f7989d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{81{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}-20t+25}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304335} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f12ce6-7e4f-5369-ae21-a8b9ddb6f8ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}-20t+5}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304336} {:answer/id #uuid "037dc31e-e1d8-5684-b347-1fac35d64cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{27{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}-t+6}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304337} {:answer/id #uuid "f31ab6e7-9c49-524a-8521-60b4a1c46bf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{85{t}^{4}-20t+25}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f17eafaa-4e17-52c4-80d4-1de331f7989d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{81{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}-20t+25}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304335} :answer-field/id #uuid "d64f3857-74e3-5fee-8a53-bba738755f86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae49ad18-0c87-5be6-839a-5bdd26096c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-123025" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=3{t}^{3}+5,\\,y={t}^{2}-5t$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=2$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{3}+5)=9{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-5t)=2t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{(9{t}^{2})^{2}+(2t-5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{81{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}-20t+25}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8469ff2-4b08-594f-b14a-113d5edfa378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+36\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289506} {:answer/id #uuid "d1082dc3-8db8-5413-9f03-5ed42be40922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+6{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289507} {:answer/id #uuid "c6474247-b26b-52e8-bc5c-50b7671013e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{10{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+12{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289508} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffecb6a-2f93-5a53-8042-e998df56cb5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289509} {:answer/id #uuid "5757d727-5d80-5732-ac89-35e82d0c08ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5757d727-5d80-5732-ac89-35e82d0c08ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289510} :answer-field/id #uuid "1329b6ad-536d-528f-b20e-bd9707ff3ace" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30eadd5a-469c-54ec-b683-aec3a996fde4" :question/math-academy-id "q-123030" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039829/assets/8868658c9bc7195f435a0109fce26b8a4fa39324c0a9a39e3fb644c5f02cdc9b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b03546f-6c79-53b9-b284-fb23ae4dba52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7352711a-225c-5f90-b63a-dc36a885a0b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+2t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289520} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c4b173-b925-5e7f-bd18-48f10587dd2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289521} {:answer/id #uuid "30f487a7-9c66-5fa1-b725-bf167f8136c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289522} {:answer/id #uuid "e22e7a8c-23a9-5fd4-87a1-c8b5b2ac2d88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289523} {:answer/id #uuid "ea76e21e-2b69-535e-81a9-5e3718e47e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+24t+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30f487a7-9c66-5fa1-b725-bf167f8136c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289522} :answer-field/id #uuid "6623aab4-4a58-5efe-bc40-75147a9ee4bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dddf8a10-d360-5144-aae4-5bd6e2bd6915" :question/math-academy-id "q-15174" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})=3{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})=3{t}^{2}-2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(3{t}^{2}+2t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2}-2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+12{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}-12{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{18{t}^{4}+8{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c609288-0e20-59b8-af5c-7962a25185bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{-3}^{1}e^{t}\\sqrt{1 + \\sin (2t)}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089563} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf5b6b1-9eb1-55f5-86ff-a7874ff80d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{-3}^{1}e^{t}\\sqrt{2 + \\cos (2t)}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089564} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fdeb25-2056-5c64-9d9d-fdadf390a7be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{-3}^{1}e^{2t}\\sqrt{1 + \\cos (2t)}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089565} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c47123-c3f4-5afa-890d-b2ae1499af5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{-3}^{1}e^{2t}\\sqrt{1 + \\sin (2t)}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089566} {:answer/id #uuid "2af5d4bd-b483-5c72-9034-abe6a7ad42f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{-3}^{1}e^{t}\\sqrt{2 + \\sin (2t)}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2af5d4bd-b483-5c72-9034-abe6a7ad42f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{-3}^{1}e^{t}\\sqrt{2 + \\sin (2t)}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089567} :answer-field/id #uuid "f47bb4c7-1764-5212-9e8c-9d67012ec3ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d674e5dc-8a57-5368-b53e-8363dba234a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-15185" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039829/assets/80577a585741748702b61596db84983592ecbc6c01fca78af033bf82dbe62ad2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={e}^{t},\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\nfrom $t=-3$ to $t=1$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5920438b-2e3b-541f-ab4e-274254545ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289534} {:answer/id #uuid "399e6721-a202-5532-b4d2-8072febd6298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289535} {:answer/id #uuid "dc35f953-44cc-5a97-9b01-08b169583ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)-4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289536} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd93796-2a27-5af2-9fd1-3d6111d06371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289537} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe1ff85-e1c9-55a4-ac69-c135a08bf481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)-2{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "399e6721-a202-5532-b4d2-8072febd6298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289535} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5703905-bd65-5e76-8460-7fa1094f93c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb7dbcac-2317-56a8-ba31-4c6afb0fd698" :question/math-academy-id "q-21339" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ddd6dde-1b3d-5e87-a217-b2480968313a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304341} {:answer/id #uuid "de23775d-a282-52e5-a955-e58e67baa09e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+3{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304342} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3a79ec-6ce4-5595-823e-72c01226248e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304343} {:answer/id #uuid "1f57bf4b-d815-5220-a948-3671c4d73bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+3{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304344} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3ff4cc-6c60-540f-bde8-1dfde9577a46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f3ff4cc-6c60-540f-bde8-1dfde9577a46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304345} :answer-field/id #uuid "421a9648-86d5-5126-b421-bc72fd96654b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2112d390-b95e-5d15-9081-2aafcdbe78a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-21347" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\nfrom $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5d95e76-6da0-59e7-ba2e-e54f6c229bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289541} {:answer/id #uuid "f95a3754-8e7a-5ac7-91b2-3967462239a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289542} {:answer/id #uuid "b72d03d8-d86a-5b26-b400-5fee85bd9c6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289543} {:answer/id #uuid "874c357f-f834-5902-ac5e-24d20b485ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289544} {:answer/id #uuid "2d21e6b1-f959-5937-95f2-768406814b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "874c357f-f834-5902-ac5e-24d20b485ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289544} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd5d6221-d149-530f-a681-b77ad15e3700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78d28f04-080c-597a-9016-2f2665e007f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-21354" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y=t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}+(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e49bcb9-e769-56f9-be28-8807dbd5ee4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304348} {:answer/id #uuid "90c9055a-bde9-591f-8fa0-e8803fc2d7bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi^2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90c9055a-bde9-591f-8fa0-e8803fc2d7bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi^2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304349} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef6f97a9-aa98-57bd-8fdb-898797af988c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b49a267a-860d-553d-868a-f2e692e29275" :question/math-academy-id "q-348086" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039829/assets/121012fc5671ada263e83ec6d3a897d187ecb3a9d56c8e4331a3c0c608a92622.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d6b098f-ac04-571f-91dc-9fdb70a2ad8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{\\pi}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d6b098f-ac04-571f-91dc-9fdb70a2ad8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{\\pi}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304352} :answer-field/id #uuid "34649362-6b33-52b0-b6a7-299b70d82198" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "515b6037-514c-5b0a-9744-97a84ce56ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-351438" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039829/assets/19f2adedfeac6e4c4af07c3d3742203020e10ce841dd22f8b2e22c36ee2bf8e1.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $L$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(${{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))}^{2}+{({e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{e}^{4t}{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}({(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}(4{cos}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}(4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{e}^{4t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5}\\,{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2beef0bb-f2de-5065-88df-6fa42f7e8cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2beef0bb-f2de-5065-88df-6fa42f7e8cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304355} :answer-field/id #uuid "9583cf61-c775-58ba-b5f7-6ed8c59ee09a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f7189a16-363d-589b-963a-795f787317a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-351471" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039829/assets/a4d2cf1820b792d804ab4d1b17ce1f36c0b63e4f406f8692d47f64a823b805f1.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t-18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡t-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9+9-18(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t-t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\ 18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =36{sin}^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-6(-1-1) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b03546f-6c79-53b9-b284-fb23ae4dba52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b9ce05e-feb5-5fe3-870a-a219dc000627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289558} {:answer/id #uuid "128635bd-e3aa-51de-a4b1-ac8a3bc5a04e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289559} {:answer/id #uuid "1adb0459-0306-56c4-9603-05ee11cc0723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{4+4t+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289560} {:answer/id #uuid "dcedce22-bb8c-59a9-9aea-2d9251e58f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289561} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb7e14b-c95b-5a70-a3cc-a409e14ba364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{4+t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "128635bd-e3aa-51de-a4b1-ac8a3bc5a04e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289559} :answer-field/id #uuid "991f5431-88a0-5a20-97fb-1e3934cbfd5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "770bbdf1-cce0-50f8-8d53-5bed9c9a42c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81377" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t-1,\\,y={t}^{2}+2$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=2$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2)=2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
